      "lifePeriodLabel": "Harriet Westbrook, Ireland, and Wales",
      "lifePeriodOrder": 3,
      "dateDisplay": "One summer-evening outing to Vauxhall in Jul 1813",
      "startDate": "1813-07-01",
      "endDate": "1813-07-31",
      "startDateApproximate": true,
      "endDateApproximate": true,
      "summary": "In the July 1813 letters printed by Hogg, Shelley invites Hogg to meet him at Cooke's Hotel because 'the N.'s have a party to Vauxhall; if you will call here at nine o'clock we will go together'. Hogg then immediately continues with a retrospective account of the Vauxhall evening itself, making the outing more than a bare plan and placing Shelley inside the social London world of that summer. Clutton-Brock's reconstruction of the same London months ties Shelley closely to the vegetarian Newton-Boinville circle, so the abbreviated 'N.' household is most plausibly the Newtons, though Hogg never expands the name.",
