      "startDate": "1818-11-05",
      "endDate": "1819-01-22",
      "startDateApproximate": true,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Mary Shelley's 22 January 1819 letter to Maria Gisborne says that 'Mr. Webb has received 200Cr. on Shelley's account' and asks Maria to 'pay yourselves and Mr. Dunn' from it, showing James Webb and Henry Dunn as practical intermediaries in the Livorno end of the Gisborne-Reveley network. Letters from Italy preserves the same commercial geography from Shelley's side when he complains that a 200-pound bill had been returned to Mr. Webb protested and asks whether temporary money could be raised at Livorno so Henry Reveley's work might continue. Jones identifies Henry Dunn as the Leghorn shopkeeper whose general store served the English community.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "By late 1818 and early 1819 Shelley relied on James Webb and Henry Dunn as Livorno intermediaries for money, parcels, and the Reveley steam project.",
      "travelMode": null,
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "medium",
