      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Claire Clairmont's journal gives the sharpest primary wording for the move, recording on 7 March 1819: 'Remove from La Villa di Parigi to Palazzo Verospi upon the Corso.' Rossetti's digest of Mary Shelley's journal preserves the same sequence, with Villa Parigi preceding the Corso lodging where the Roman months unfolded. The Cimitero Acattolico newsletter notes the plaque on Palazzo Verospi at Via del Corso 375 commemorating the Shelleys' stay in 1819; the plaque inscription itself identifies this house as the place where Shelley wrote Prometheus Unbound and The Cenci. Mary Shelley's 1839 notes independently confirm the Roman domestic setting of the months in which Shelley worked on those writings. Mary Shelley's journal then adds a crucial chronological limit by noting the move on 7 May 1819 to 65 Via Sistina, next door to Miss Curran.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "Palazzo Verospi is the best-attested Corso lodging for the Roman household between early March and early May 1819.",
