      "summary": "Nicoletta Caputo's profile of Margaret King Moore says that Mr and Mrs Mason lived in Casa Silva, in the old Via Mala Gonella, now identified either with Via Sancasciani or the adjacent Via Pietro Gori. Mary Shelley's journal gives the primary rhythm behind that statement: on 27 January 1820 she spent the day with Mrs Mason, on 31 January Mrs Mason with Laurette and Nerina called on the Shelleys, and through February the family repeatedly dined, drank tea, or spent evenings at Casa Silva. Claire Clairmont's journal adds the daughters' daily presence there, including walks and drives with Laurette and Nerina and repeated calls during Nerina's measles. Together the journals show Casa Silva as a real lived household, not just a later scholarly identification. Will Bowers's Cambridge study adds why that mattered politically: Mrs Mason was a former pupil of Wollstonecraft, a self-described United Irishwoman, and one of the pre-existing radical expatriates who made early-1820 Pisa a city of exiles rather than a neutral social refuge.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "Casa Silva was one of the first doors through which the Shelleys entered the Pisan circle and, through Mrs Mason, a radical expatriate world linked to Wollstonecraft, Paine, and the Vacca Berlinghieri household.",
      "travelMode": null,
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "high",
      "works": [

      ],
      "companions": [
        "Mary Shelley",
        "Claire Clairmont",
        "Margaret King Moore",
        "George William Tighe",
        "Anna Laura Georgina Tighe ('Laurette')",
        "Catherine Elizabeth Louisa Tighe ('Nerina')"
      ],
      "companionsNote": "Mary and Claire's journals make Casa Silva a real household scene in early 1820: Mrs Mason, George William Tighe, and the daughters Laurette and Nerina all belong to the social circle through which Percy was drawn toward Dr. Vacca and the wider Pisan world. Bowers also makes Casa Silva part of the pre-existing radical expatriate Pisa into which the Shelleys arrived.",
