      "startDateApproximate": true,
      "endDateApproximate": true,
      "summary": "The Liberal Project places the Shelleys' return to Pisa in late January 1820 at Palazzo dei Galletti on Lungarno Pacinotti, adding that Shelley, Mary, Claire Clairmont, Percy Florence, and Thomas Medwin all spent part of this period there. The Comune di Pisa's Palazzo Chiesa page says the palace where they first lodged in 1820 is the building that now houses the Department of Political Science, and current Universita di Pisa pages place that complex at Lungarno Pacinotti 43/44. Editorial notes to Mary Shelley's journal add that the winter at Florence had been severe, that the climate did not suit Shelley, and that the move to Pisa both brought the family nearer to Mrs Mason and enabled Shelley to consult Andrea Vacca, whom Mrs Mason had recommended. Jones's 1955 Maria Gisborne letters article likewise says that the move to Pisa on 26 January 1820 happened principally because Shelley wished to consult Vacca Berlinghieri about his health; later notes for 1821 describe Vacca as diagnosing a disease of the kidneys while Shelley himself feared he might have to be 'cut for the stone'.",
