      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "The Liberal Project, drawing on Mary Shelley's note on the poems of 1820, places the Shelleys in the absent Gisbornes' house, Casa Ricci, from 15 June to 5 August 1820 and identifies its probable site at 59 Viale Guglielmo Marconi. Claire Clairmont's correspondence appendix independently says that from 15 June to 3 August 1820 the whole family moved into the Gisbornes' vacant house in Leghorn during the Paolo Foggi crisis, while Ingpen's 1914 edition of the Shelley letters also describes the poetical Letter to Maria Gisborne as written from the Gisbornes' house at Leghorn lent to Shelley during their temporary absence in England. Steven E. Jones's 2019 reading makes the physical setting more precise: Shelley wrote the poem in Henry Reveley's workshop or study within Casa Ricci, surrounded by mechanical parts for the projected 44-foot steamboat intended to connect Livorno, Genoa, and Marseilles. Shelley's Letter to Maria Gisborne is dated from Leghorn on 1 July 1820 and belongs to this stay.",
