      "summary": "Percy Shelley in Context treats 'The Boat on the Serchio' as a celebration of the joys of boating and friendship in Shelley's final Italian period, making the Serchio a specific lived landscape of the Pisan exile. Mary Shelley's journal gives the practical boating background: after the canal accident of 16 April 1821 on the return from Leghorn, Henry Reveley brought the repaired boat back on 30 April and the first days of May were devoted to sailing, before later summer trips down the Serchio with Edward Williams fed the poem's social and riverine world.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": null,
      "travelMode": "By boat on the river.",
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "medium",
      "works": [
        "The Boat on the Serchio"
      ],
      "companions": [
        "Edward Ellerker Williams",
        "Dominic the boatman"
      ],
      "companionsNote": "Edward Williams is the poem's named boat companion under the figure of Melchior; Dominic appears as the boatman in the poem itself.",
