      "lifePeriodOrder": 9,
      "dateDisplay": "Byron's Pisa residence, visited from late Oct 1821 to 24 Mar 1822",
      "startDate": "1821-10-25",
      "endDate": "1822-03-24",
      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Mary Shelley's 30 November 1821 letter says that the Shelleys were living at Tre Palazzi di Chiesa with Lord Byron 'just opposite to us in Casa Lanfranchi', establishing the poet's Pisa residence across the Arno from the Shelley household. Byron's published letters and journals preserve the same Pisan domestic phase from his side. The Archivio di Stato di Pisa identifies its present main seat, Palazzo Toscanelli on Lungarno Mediceo 17, as the same Lanfranchi palace rented by Byron in 1821-1822, and the Comune di Pisa's heritage page likewise describes the building as Palazzo Toscanelli, already Palazzo Lanfranchi. Mary Shelley's 6 April 1822 narrative of the Masi affray confirms that Shelley entered or returned to that same house on 24 March 1822, and the Bodleian description of her 12 April letter to Medwin shows that the judicial aftermath around Casa Lanfranchi involved Teresa Guiccioli's household and Byron's servant Tita [Giovanni Falcieri]. C. L. Cline's historical study of the Pisan circle remains one of the fullest reconstructions of the same Byron-household phase in Pisa.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "Before and after the Masi affray, Byron's residence in Pisa was this palace on Lungarno Mediceo, today Palazzo Toscanelli and seat of the Archivio di Stato.",
