      "lifePeriodOrder": 9,
      "dateDisplay": "24 Mar 1822, on the ride before the Masi affray",
      "startDate": "1822-03-24",
      "endDate": "1822-03-24",
      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Thomas Moore's 1830 life of Byron says that by the spring of 1822 Byron had shifted his daily Pisa ride to the road leading east from Porta alla Spiaggia, where the party dismounted at a podere for pistol practice before returning to town. The editorial note to Mary Shelley's journal for 7 December 1821 identifies that 'Garden' more closely as Villa La Podera at Cisanello, about two miles outside Pisa, and adds that after the Marchese Viviani forbade Byron to use firearms in the gardens of Palazzo Lanfranchi it was probably Andrea Vacca who arranged for the party to shoot there instead. Leslie Marchand's Byron biography makes the secondary identification cleaner still, saying that Byron made arrangements with 'some friends of Dr. Vacca, the Castinelli family,' who lived outside the walls, and that the rides then went out almost daily to Villa la Podera at Cisanello. Pisa Tours identifies Viale delle Piagge as the later public promenade that extended from the demolished gate toward the eastern riverside.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": null,
      "travelMode": "Horseback on the outward ride, while Mary Shelley and Teresa Guiccioli followed in a carriage.",
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "medium",
      "works": [

      ],
      "companions": [
        "Lord Byron",
        "Edward John Trelawny",
        "Captain John Hay",
        "Pietro Gamba",
        "John Taaffe"
      ],
      "companionsNote": "These one-day Pisa records belong to the ride and affray of 24 March 1822, in which Shelley was part of Byron's mounted party.",
