      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Mary Shelley's 30 November 1821 letter places Byron 'just opposite to us in Casa Lanfranchi', and her 6 April 1822 narrative says that after the clash Byron rode back to his own house for arms and that Masi was wounded while passing Casa Lanfranchi. The same published letter adds that when the household gathered there after the affray, suspicion fell on one of Byron's servants and on Teresa Guiccioli's servant Antonio; the Bodleian descriptions of Mary's 12 April 1822 letter to Medwin and Claire Clairmont's 9 April 1822 letter to Mary identify the imprisoned Byron servant as Tita [Giovanni Falcieri] and confirm Teresa Guiccioli's presence in the affair's immediate aftermath. C. L. Cline's study of the Pisan circle remains a major extended reconstruction of the Masi crisis and its Byron-household setting. The Archivio di Stato di Pisa identifies the present Palazzo Toscanelli on Lungarno Mediceo 17 as Byron's former residence.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "Byron rode back here for arms after the affray, and the judicial aftermath swept up Byron's servant Tita and Teresa Guiccioli's servant Antonio as suspected participants.",
      "travelMode": null,
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "high",
      "works": [

      ],
