      "startDate": "1822-07-08",
      "endDate": "1822-07-08",
      "startDateApproximate": false,
      "endDateApproximate": false,
      "summary": "Percy Shelley in Context says that Shelley drowned on 8 July 1822 in the Gulf of La Spezia, just west of Viareggio, during the return voyage from Livorno toward Lerici; the Morgan copy of the 4 July 1822 letter to Jane Williams provides immediate pre-voyage evidence for the Pisa-Livorno-Lerici movement, and Mary Shelley's Volume II account preserves Captain Roberts's testimony that he watched the boat from the Leghorn mole until storm haze hid it from view. Walter Edwin Peck adds the practical details that Shelley had retained only the young sailor Charles Vivian aboard the Ariel, earlier christened Don Juan, after sending back the two older seamen who had brought her from Genoa. Donald Prell's Roberts biography further records that the wrecked boat was raised in September 1822, bought by Roberts, later altered by decking, and remembered in Roberts's 1834 journal as finally lost in Corfu.",
      "inscription": null,
      "importantFact": "Shelley drowned here on 8 July 1822 during the return voyage from Livorno toward Lerici, aboard the Don Juan or Ariel with Edward Williams and Charles Vivian.",
      "travelMode": "By sailing boat on the return from Livorno toward Lerici.",
      "routeNote": null,
      "certainty": "high",
